        <div id="what-it-is" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What a Power Vent Water Heater Actually Is
          </h2>

          <p>
            A power vent water heater is an ordinary tank-type gas water heater with a fan bolted to the top. The fan, which manufacturers call a blower, pulls hot combustion gases out of the flue and pushes them through plastic pipe to the outside. That one part changes where the heater can go. A standard atmospheric heater relies on hot exhaust rising up a metal flue into a chimney, so it has to sit under a chimney or a vertical vent through the roof. A power vent heater does not need either. It can go in a utility room, a finished basement or a closet, as long as a pipe can reach an outside wall.
          </p>

          <p>
            The blower is also why the vent pipe can be plastic. By the time the fan has mixed room air into the exhaust, it is cool enough for PVC, ABS or CPVC, according to both Rheem's and A.O. Smith's specifications, instead of the double-wall metal pipe a natural-draft heater needs. Plastic pipe is cheap, easy to cut and can run sideways, which is what makes a sidewall exit practical. Most installs go out through the rim joist a few feet above the outside grade.
          </p>

          <p>
            What a power vent unit does not change is where combustion air comes from. A plain power vent draws its air from the room it sits in, just as an atmospheric heater does. Rheem's literature says so directly: the heater uses indoor air for combustion and the blower exhausts the flue gases. So the room still needs enough air, and a tight utility closet may still need louvered doors or a combustion-air opening. That is the difference between power vent and power direct vent, covered in the next section.
          </p>

          <p>
            The fan needs electricity. Every heater on this page needs a standard 120V outlet, usually within reach of the cord that comes on the blower. The burner, tank and gas control are the same as a non-powered heater, and so is most of the maintenance: flushing sediment, checking the anode and testing the relief valve. The blower adds one more part that can fail, and blower assemblies are sold as a separate replacement part for when one fails out of warranty.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are still deciding between tank, tankless and heat pump, start with our <a href="/types-of-water-heaters/">guide to the types of water heaters</a>. If you already know you want a gas tank and are choosing between venting styles, our <a href="/best-gas-water-heater/">best gas water heaters</a> guide covers atmospheric and condensing models alongside power vent. This page is only about the power vent decision: whether you need one, which venting rules will decide the install, and which models are worth buying online.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vent-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
