                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Atmospheric</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Rises naturally up a chimney or B-vent</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Room air</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Metal flue to chimney</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Power vent</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Blower pushes it out a wall or roof</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Room air</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes, 120V</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2-4 in. PVC, ABS or CPVC</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Direct vent</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Natural draft through a short wall vent</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Outside air via concentric vent</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Manufacturer vent kit</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Power direct vent</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Blower, two pipes or concentric</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Outside air</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes, 120V</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Two plastic pipes</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Condensing</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Blower, cool exhaust and condensate drain</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Usually outside air</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Plastic pipe plus drain</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Atmospheric is the cheapest and simplest option, and if the old heater is atmospheric and the chimney is sound, it is usually the right replacement. No fan, no outlet and no new wall hole, and it keeps working in a power cut as long as it has a standing pilot. The catch is the chimney. An older masonry chimney that used to be shared with a furnace may be oversized once a high-efficiency furnace moves to its own plastic vent, and an orphaned water heater on a big flue can draft badly. That situation is one of the most common reasons plumbers recommend switching to power vent.
          </p>

          <p>
            Direct vent heaters, which are not powered, pull outside air through a concentric vent and push exhaust out by natural draft. They suit a heater that sits right against an outside wall, but the vent run is usually very short and limited to the manufacturer's kit. Power direct vent adds a blower to that design, taking outside air through one pipe and exhausting through another. It costs more than plain power vent, but it is the right choice for a sealed closet or a very airtight house where room air is scarce.
          </p>

          <p>
            Condensing tank heaters go further. They extract so much heat that the exhaust condenses, which means higher efficiency, plastic venting and a condensate drain. They cost much more and are a different purchase, closer to a high-efficiency furnace than a basic tank. For most households replacing a failed heater, plain power vent is the middle ground: no chimney needed, cheap plastic pipe, and a price close to an atmospheric tank.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Buy power vent when the chimney is the problem.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If the old heater vents into a good chimney, an atmospheric replacement is cheaper and works without power. Power vent earns its premium when there is no chimney, the chimney is orphaned or oversized, or the heater needs to move.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

