        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Buy a power vent water heater when the chimney is the problem: none at all, an orphaned flue that drafts badly, or a heater that needs to move. Then let the vent table pick the model. The A.O. Smith GPVX-50L delivers the most hot water of any 50-gallon power vent here, but only on short runs; the Rheem Professional Classic models reach 150 equivalent feet on 3-inch pipe.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Whichever you choose, plan the outlet, the wall termination and the blackout fallback before the old heater comes out. If the chimney is sound and outages are common, an atmospheric heater is still the cheaper and more resilient replacement.
          </p>
